alytica then turned  around and built software that was  specifically designed to influence  people's choices in elections i don't  think i need to explain why this is  morally shady and how it interferes with  free and fair elections and while it  wasn't facebook themselves that was  engaging in such activities it was their  platform that allowed this to even take  place  in fact facebook themselves confirmed  that cambridge analytica did not break  any policies when it comes to collecting  this data it wasn't until they  transferred the data out of facebook  that they broke facebook's policies as  you would guess people nor prosecutors  were exactly convinced by this reasoning  and they would crack down on facebook  hard  too often we see companies that have  committed terrible crimes walk away with  measly 100 or 200 million fines which is  insignificant compared to their profit  but with facebook the ftc imposed a 5  billion fine not only was this a  record-breaking fine but it was 20 times  larger than the previous largest privacy  and data security penalty now of course  a lot of y'all will argue that even this  is not enough for facebook but the ftc  did try their best and though facebook  was able to survive this nightmare user  sentiment has never been the same  in fact even individuals who have  literally made billions from facebook  have turned on their company including  whatsapp and instagram's founders likely  the most outspoken of this bunch is  whatsapp's co-founder brian acton whose  trade-up advocates for people to delete  facebook  given that such figures have turned on  facebook it's not surprising that stock  investors have also turned on facebook  even investors who are fundamentally  bullish on the company are cautious of  investing in the stock simply because  they don't want to hold such a  controversial company and unless  facebook changes this overwhelming  negative sentiment against them i think  it's going to continue to be quite a  difficult uphill battle for them  a lack of user growth paired with  atrocious user sentiment is already bad  enough but the nail in the coffin for  facebook was apple's privacy changes  last year in april of 2021 apple started  giving users the option to choose  whether they wanted to let apps track  their activity and as you would guess  basically everyone chose to prevent apps  from tracking their activity within just  a few weeks of this feature launching 96  percent of users opted out of app  tracking and honestly i think that the  four percent that did allow tracking  might have pressed a lot accidentally  and given that 97 of facebook's revenue  comes from advertising it this was  extremely bad news in fact facebook  projects that apple's privacy changes  will cost them 10 billion revenue just  this year at first facebook tried to  fight against these changes they tried  to convince people to press allow and  there were even rumors suggesting that  facebook was preparing an anti-trust  lawsuit against apple but i think  facebook eventually came to a  realization this was a battle that they  could never really win even if they took  apple to court and won the lawsuit this  would just make users resent them even  more leading to even greater losses in  revenue instead it looks like facebook  has chosen to take a similar stance to  snapchat ceo evan spiegel evan insists  that even though these changes are  brutal for the business in the short  term it's needed for the industry in the  long term and this is a very important  point because the truth is apple's  privacy changes last year are likely  just the first of many ten years ago  most people didn't know how companies  like facebook and google made their  money and frankly they didn't care they  were able to use a great service for  free and that's all they cared about but  as more of these quote-unquote free  services into the market people have  become quite aware of the inner workings  of these companies and most people  aren't big fans this has caused privacy  to become a focal point amongst  customers and the newer social media  companies aren't focusing on being free  but rather being private take signal for  example signo is a non-profit encrypted  messaging service created by one of  whatsapp's co-founders brian acton and  as of the end of 2021 it has a total of  40 million active monthly users now of  course this is just a small percentage  compared to whatsapp's 2 billion active  monthly users but with people like elon  musk supporting signal i think signal's  user count is only going up also i don't  think signal is just some anomaly either  rather i think it's the beginning of a  trend towards private social media  services which again spells disaster for  facebook  despite all the shortfalls surrounding  facebook's social media business all  hope is not lost for the company given  the enormous size of facebook they have  at least 10 years to figure something  out and change the direction of the  company and given that mark has been  working on steering facebook away from  social media for years now it looks like  he's on top of this issue he even went  as far as changing the entire company's  name which is usually something you  never want to do for brand recognition  purposes i guess you realize that their  brand facebook wasn't doing the company  any good  looking forward it it looks like the  main goal seems to be growing their vr  business and the metaverse which is a  pretty solid bet given that this does  seem to be the next big thing it looks  like even apple is betting big on ar and  vr technology but something to note is  that even if vr is the next big thing  there's no guarantees that facebook will  come out on top just because they're the  first people there  after all facebook themselves was not  the first social media company in the  world with that being said though being  large and the first does have its  advantages so we'll just have to wait  